G9 GSM TYPEWRITER

- From: 200/201, 203, 206-come-out, s04/s05/s07/s10; tools gsmline.py,
  stanzarms.py, gsmverse.py.
- Sound: a speech-codec drum machine and chant made of typed words; with
  gsmverse.py, a synthetic spoken phrase.
- Mechanism: a comment line "# " + 30 chars + \n = one 33-byte GSM frame:
  3-letter throat (usable: bib, imp, hah, pea, ham, pal, amp), then four
  7-letter subframes, first letter = LTP lag (odd rings, even damps), second =
  loudness (a=ppp … z=fff, space = rest). The piece reads itself: -f gsm
  -sample_rate R -i "subfile,,start,S,end,E,,:$0". gsmverse.py (206) builds
  syllables (attack/sustain/decay/gap) by choosing dictionary words by their
  second letter.
- Instrument: in: a stanza (text) + rate (stanza grooves at 880–7048; speech at
  6000; single lines as tones at 8250–28160). Out: audio. Hygiene:
  asetpts=N/SR/TB on every decode, aformat=channel_layouts=mono before
  join/amerge/AAC. Text authoring is a poetic form (the handoff's live thread: a
  stanza that is real English and a groove).
- Coupling: low. Live: no. The composer is least sure of 206 by ear: GSM at
  speech rate saturates.
- Neighbouring grammars (200 handoff): G.723.1 frame type is the first byte's
  low 2 bits (space/d/h/l/p/t/x = 24-byte speech, a/e/i/m/q/u/y = 20-byte,
  b/f/j/n/r/v/z = SID, c/g/k/o/s/w = 1-byte erasure), so spelling is a rhythm
  (untouched). A file starting #!AMR is both a shell script and an auto-probed
  AMR-NB stream (untested as music).

G10 GSM VOCAL TRACT / CODEC-FRAME OSCILLATOR

- From: 200/s01, 201 (tones), 203 (settle + hold), journal (G.729);
  400/405-vox-humana, x08-gsm-vowel-pipe; frame packer
  studios/400/tools/gsmframes.py (my first snapshot said the packer was
  missing; it is now in the repo).
- Sound: a steady vowel-coloured voice; in 405 a chorale singing the first
  paragraph of ffmpeg's manual.
- Mechanism: either (a) typed frame: one text frame looped = pitch rate/160 with
  its words' vowel; or (b) designed frame (NOTEBOOK, 400): 33 bytes packed by
  hand (magic 0xD, 8 LARs of 6,6,5,5,4,4,3,3 bits, 4 sub-frames of
  lag/gain/grid/xmax + 13×3-bit pulses). LARs come from a vowel LPC designed at
  8 kHz (designing at the declared rate saturates the lattice). One pulse code 7
  per sub-frame gives a glottal pulse every 40 samples, so pitch = declared
  rate/40 and formants scale with pitch. xmaxc ≈ 10–14 avoids clipping. The
  output is always 160-periodic, which adds an undertone two octaves down (kept
  as a feature). "Hold, don't loop" (200): decode with -stream_loop 49, cut one
  settled period (atrim=start_sample=K·P:end_sample=(K+1)·P), aloop. This is
  faster and exact, and avoids the -stream_loop -1 timestamp bug.
- Instrument: in: vowel (or text) + pitch. Out: audio. 405's sing FRAME RATE
  DELAY LEN FADEOUT_AT GAIN L R is already the interface. Coupling: low. Live:
  no (pitch is a demuxer rate; a chorale needs one input per note, 83 in 405).
  400's live thread: "a GSM opera" (consonants as noise pulse patterns,
  LTP-sustained pitch, vibrato by alternating frames).

