4. SHARED PLUMBING (NOT INSTRUMENTS, BUT A LIBRARY NEEDS ALL OF IT)

- spectrumsynth front-end. Input format=gray16 (value/65535 = linear magnitude;
  phase = cycles). gray/grayf32/yuv and anything through rotate get converted to
  limited-range YUV: a 16/255 offset that gates quiet pixels and adds a hiss
  floor. Row 0 from the bottom is DC. Default win_func is rect; use hann unless
  T1's rect is wanted. Do warps in geq p(x,y) (bilinear). Mono output needs
  aformat=channel_layouts=mono.
- Phase images. Fullframe (600): 65535*mod(k*(x+2)/4,1) makes steady rows
  coherent sines and single columns centred clicks. Scroll (300):
  255*mod(bin*N/4,1). Constant phase buzzes at the hop rate (200) and makes a
  hidden comb where only every 4th bin is coherent (300). Crest factor (300):
  add a fixed per-bin hash + sin(bin*12.9898)*43758.5453 or struck chords align
  into +8 dBFS spikes (crest 22 → 15 dB) and the limiter erases attacks. Random
  phase per column = clicks (100); a fixed hash per bin is fine. A small per-row
  hash above ~300 Hz on one ear only gives width (600).
- Peak-pick p*gt(p,p(X,Y-1))*gte(p,p(X,Y+1)) after any blur, interpolation,
  codec or stretch.
- Voice compiler (100): complex accumulation in st(8)/st(9) + placeholder
  templates; parenthesise each voice.
- Instrument functions already written (the library's prototype): 400 pipe organ
  sing mov tab bus gate singer, 500 room box C, 100 templates, 200 s06 chain,
  600 shared SCORE/ROT/RD strings, and instruments/diatonic-live.sh with its
  @knob header convention that perform.py parses.
- Expression engine: 10 registers (st(10+) aliases 9, silently); ~99 flat ;
  terms ("Cannot allocate memory"); random(i) reseeds from register i; while()
  works per sample and if() is lazy; no neq(); pack state as digits/bit-fields
  of one double (306: seven 7-bit values). In geq index by T, not N.
- Loopbacks: label/map the final output; apad=whole_dur + common atrim; -map
  every output explicitly; add fps=R after a video [dec:N]; aligned codecs:
  mp2/mp3/aac/vorbis/adpcm/g726 (opus 8k, wma, speex are not).
- Raw/codec inputs: asetpts=N/SR/TB after every looped or custom-rate decode
  (else timestamps break or renders hang); GSM has no channel layout; don't use
  amovie loop=0; aloop needs atrim+asetpts first for start>0; "hold, don't loop"
  (G10).
- Level discipline: alimiter=level=0 everywhere (every studio rediscovered it);
  afir irnorm=2; per-stem astats/section measurement (600 sect.sh); around −14
  LUFS, peaks < −1 dB; -y in every piece (500); bass mono.
- Ears for blind composers (each studio built its own): peak lists with note
  names (200/300/400/500), Goertzel envelope per partial (300 env.py),
  zoom-by-resampling spectrograms (300 spec.sh, 500 look.sh), multi-resolution
  log viewer (400 pitchview.py), onset autocorrelation for groove (200
  groove.py). A shared "ear" kit is as much a library need as the instruments.

5. SETTLE FIRST: CONTRADICTIONS BETWEEN STUDIOS

These should be resolved by measurement before anything in the spectral family
becomes a library instrument.
1. spectrumsynth FFT size. 100 writes FFT = 2h (32768 rows → 65536, bin =
   sr/2h). 300, 600 and the original NOTEBOOK entry write 2(h−1) (513 → 1024,
   2049 → 4096). T1's tuning (A = harmonic 256 at sr 28160) depends on which is
   true.
2. Which heights are fast. 100: "powers of two": h=4096 and 32768 fast, h=4097
   (and 1025, 2050) ~300× slower. 300: h=2049 fast (10 s in ~1 s), 1025 9 s,
   4097 34–48 s, 8193 27 s, 513 0.7 s. 600: 513 fast, 1025 ~16× slower. They
   agree that 513 is fast and 1025/4097 slow, but disagree on the rule: 2049 is
   not a power of two, yet 300 finds it fast; 100 finds 4096 fast. One benchmark
   sweep of h ∈ {513, 1024, 1025, 2048, 2049, 4096, 4097, 32768} settles it.
3. Row/bin offset. 200's typewriter organ found "the bin mapping after transpose
   was 2 off". It is probably the same question as #1 (inferred).
4. rotate on spectra. 600 calls rotate a dead end (forced limited-range YUV,
   hiss). The proposal lists rotate angle as the live control for the
   quarter-turn drop. Test whether rotate on gray16 (or with
   out_range/full-range forcing) keeps black at zero.
5. The surround upmixer. 500's own day-1 verdict listed surround among repair
   tools that "mostly pass tonal material or turn it to noise"; later the same
   studio found it is the consonance detector (506). The difference is the input
   (one voice per channel, phase-locked partials). The dead-end list should be
   read with that caveat.
6. Brightness scales. 100 uses scale=log with 255 = a full-scale sine
   (brightness = 255 + 2.125·dB); 300 says pixel 200 at scale=lin clips and uses
   ~40 per partial; 600 uses gray16 values up to 40000/65535 at lin; 100's 105
   uses lin with 180·amplitude. A single level table (dBFS per pixel value, per
   scale, per height) is needed.
7. Loop mechanics. -stream_loop -1 on subfiles at non-default rates gives wrong
   timestamps (200); DFPWM with -stream_loop drifts ~1% (200); amovie loop=0
   hangs (400); aloop with start>0 doesn't loop (400). Not contradictions, but
   four studios each found a different failure. The library should have one
   blessed "hold a period" recipe (G10).
8. Old stats. Per-band numbers from analyze.sh before studio 600's fix are
   unreliable. 000's journal has since withdrawn its "weak mids" verdict as an
   artifact; any other mix judgment made from pre-fix stats should be
   re-measured.
9. Live in real time vs timeline. My first snapshot said real-time control
   needed libzmq; the stdin console (verified twice) makes that moot. The
   remaining open question is latency: one aeval frame (~21 ms at 1024 samples)
   plus the device buffer, and whether loopback/minterpolate graphs keep up in
   real time (untested).

